{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Therefore say.</b> From here is an allusion to the priestly blessing, in which it is written (Numbers 6): אמור להם." "<b>Behold, I give him My covenant of peace.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): He promised him a covenant of peace, that he need not fear the brothers of Zimri, that they would not avenge his vengeance. And what would his reward be? — והיתה לו ולזרעו אחריו ברית כהונת עולם — 'and it shall be for him and his seed after him a covenant of everlasting priesthood,' that the priesthood should be his eternally." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: הנני נותן לו את בריתי שלום — the ו״ו of שלום is cut, to teach you that the covenant does not belong to a priest unless he is whole, without blemish; but if he lacks anything, he is disqualified — thus they expounded in the chapter האומר in Qiddushin. And so we find in the Masorah: the ו״ו of שלום is small." "<b>And I have heard</b> that the word is a notarikon: שלום = שלא למום — 'without blemish.' And this supports the tradition of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, that this Pinḥas is Elijah. And so we find in his own case, that he said (I Kings 19:4): ״קח נא את נפשי ממני כי לא טוב אנכי מאבותי״ — that is, than my fathers who died; and because he rejected the life of this world, He gave him the life of the world to come, and he ascended in a storm to heaven." "<b>By way of</b> the Kabbalah: הנני נותן לו את בריתי שלום — He promised him the gift of the attribute called ברית, which is כנסת ישראל, and it is the attribute of judgment; and this attribute was to be his, in the manner of what they said of Abraham — בת היתה לו, 'a daughter he had' — and therefore they said here והיתה לו ברית, 'and it shall be to him for a covenant' — for the ברית shall be his. And He promised him that the שלום would cleave to the ברית, in the language of (Leviticus 26:6): ״ונתתי שלום בארץ״. And from here it was fitting that he should live forever; therefore it is written (Malachi 2:5): ״בריתי היתה אתו החיים והשלום״ — for life is from the side of mercy. And this is what it says: אשר קנא לאלהיו — in the language of (Psalms 85): ״צדק ושלום נשקו״, which is the supernal union through which the world is sustained; corresponding to it, in the lower Creation, is the matter of fitting union and coupling. Therefore when Pinḥas saw a coupling that was unfitting, he was zealous. And from here you will understand the attribute of שלום that He gave him: that it was measure for measure." "<b>It may be fitting</b> to say that because in the end he departed and was taken away, his godly name is called Eliyahu (אליהו), which embraces two Names — judgment and mercy: אל is judgment and it is the covenant, יו״ד ה״א וא״ו (i.e., the letters of יהוה); it is mercy and it is peace. And so he is comprised of the force of judgment and the force of mercy, and this is 'My covenant of peace' (בריתי שלום). Therefore he punished the officer of the fifty and his fifty by the force of judgment, and he revived the dead woman by the force of mercy. And further there is a hint in the reckoning of his name: it is double (פי שנים) in the special Name; and Pinchas was the seventh generation to Yaakov our father, just as Chanoch was seventh to Adam, and they correspond to the seventh day, which is Shabbat, which is the day of rest and of life — and this is clear."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:36:16.763137267Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}